{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"gpu","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":16565361}],"dockerImageVersionId":31400,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":true}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/nahuelfuentes/building-a-knowledge-graph-from-textbooks-with-nlp?scriptVersionId=324395480\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import spacy\nfrom spacy import displacy\nimport en_core_web_sm","metadata":{"_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T05:53:29.251452Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T05:53:29.251731Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-04T05:53:35.926298Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-04T05:53:29.251707Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-04T05:53:35.925675Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":1},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"An NLP pipeline that extracts concepts and relationships from textbook pages and structures them as queryable knowledge graph\n","metadata":{},"attachments":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Ingest\n\nIn this section we will load and tokenize textbook examples for generating the Knowledge Graph, we will extract the text with OCR because the dataset we are using has some text thats difficult to parse because of encoding issues so we rely on OCR to scan the PDF.\n\nFor production enviroments it could be change to a more delicate and efficient parser but that's outside the scope of this notebook.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"!pip uninstall fitz -y\n!pip install pymupdf --quiet\n\n!pip install pytesseract pillow\n!apt-get install -y tesseract-ocr --quiet\n\nimport pytesseract\nfrom PIL import Image\nimport fitz\n\ndef clean_text(text):\n    import re\n    text = re.sub(r'\\s+', ' ', text)\n    return text.strip()\n\ndef extract_with_ocr(pdf_path):\n    doc = fitz.open(pdf_path)\n    sentences = []\n    \n    for page_num, page in enumerate(doc):\n        \n        pix = page.get_pixmap(dpi=300)\n        img = Image.frombytes(\"RGB\", [pix.width, pix.height], pix.samples)\n        \n        text = pytesseract.image_to_string(img)\n        text = clean_text(text)\n        \n        spacy_doc = nlp(text)\n        for sent in spacy_doc.sents:\n            clean = sent.text.strip()\n            if clean:\n                sentences.append({\"page\": page_num + 1, \"text\": clean})\n    \n    return sentences\n","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T05:53:35.927646Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T05:53:35.928074Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-04T05:53:50.068659Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-04T05:53:35.928043Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-04T05:53:50.068034Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\u001b[33mWARNING: Skipping fitz as it is not installed.\u001b[0m\u001b[33m\n\u001b[2K   \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m25.0/25.0 MB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m64.3 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0m:00:01\u001b[0m00:01\u001b[0m\n\u001b[?25hRequirement already satisfied: pytesseract in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (0.3.13)\nRequirement already satisfied: pillow in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (11.3.0)\nRequirement already satisfied: packaging>=21.3 in /usr/local/lib/python3.12/dist-packages (from pytesseract) (26.2)\nReading package lists...\nBuilding dependency tree...\nReading state information...\ntesseract-ocr is already the newest version (4.1.1-2.1build1).\n0 upgraded, 0 newly installed, 0 to remove and 141 not upgraded.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":2},{"cell_type":"code","source":"nlp = spacy.load(\"en_core_web_sm\")\n\nsentences = extract_with_ocr(\"/kaggle/input/datasets/nahuelfuentes/textbook-example/textbook-chapter-1.pdf\")\n\nprint(f\"Total sentence:{len(sentences)}\")\nprint(sentences[0])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T05:53:50.06993Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T05:53:50.070536Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Extraction","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Concepts","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"First we need to capture the concepts in the textbooks, we can't use plain **SpaCy** for this because the *EN* captures proper nouns such as people or places, but can't capture concepts that are plain nouns. So we need to train a *NER* to label this nouns as `CONCEPT`, maybe generating a custom dataset or manually checking if some nouns are concepts or no.\n\nAs an example let's see what entities are recognized in some sentences of the text","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"sample = sentences[0:10]\n\nsample_text = ' '.join([s['text'] for s in sample])\n\ndisplacy.render(nlp(sample_text), jupyter=True, style='ent')","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We see that raw **NLP** can't capture concepts such as *digital circuits* or *logic circuits*, but if we look at the token level it can capture this tokens, we need a way to capture the candidates of tokens to become concepts","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(f\"Token \\t\\t\\tLemma \\t\\t\\tStopword \\tPOS\".format('Token', 'Lemma', 'Stopword', 'POS'))\nprint(\"-\"*80)\nfor sentence in sentences[3:5]:\n    sentence_tokenized = nlp(sentence['text'])\n    for token in sentence_tokenized:\n        print(f\"{str(token).ljust(10)}\\t\\t{token.lemma_.ljust(10)}\\t\\t{token.is_stop}\\t\\t{token.pos_}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"It captured *circuits* as `NOUN` and *logic* both as `NOUN` and `ADJ` in differente tokens. Based on this, we try to filter tokens that are `NOUN`,`PROPN` and `ADJ`, they aren't a Stopword and they length is greater than, say, 3.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for sentence in sentences[3:5]:\n    sentence_tokenized = nlp(sentence['text'])\n    for token in sentence_tokenized:\n        if (token.pos_ in ['NOUN','PROPN','ADJ'] and not token.is_stop and len(token.lemma_) > 3):\n                print(token.lemma_)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"It's seeing a lot better for our candidates, but some of them are repetead and some concepts are span tokens such as *logic circuit* and *digital circuit*, we will use noun chunks to capture them","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for sentence in sentences[3:5]:\n    sentence_tokenized = nlp(sentence['text'])\n    for chunk in sentence_tokenized.noun_chunks:\n        print(chunk.lemma_)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Much better, we can capture *logic circuit* and *digital circuit*, let's define a cleaning function for noun chunks based on what we now know. To filter duplicates, we simply use a set to catch plurals and, as shown before, generic terms such as *thing*, *term*, *kind*, etc.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\n\nGENERIC_TERMS = {'think', 'term', 'way', 'type', 'kind', 'part', 'example'}\n\ndef clean_chunk(chunk):\n    lemma = chunk.lemma_.lower()\n    lemma = re.sub(r'^(the|a|an|this|these|those|which|they)\\s+', '', lemma)\n    lemma = lemma.replace('\"','').strip()\n    return lemma\n\ndef is_valid_chunk(chunk):\n    lemma = clean_chunk(chunk)\n    if len(lemma) < 4: # Drop nouns with less than 4 characters\n        return False\n    if chunk.root.pos_ == 'PRON': # Drop if they come from a pronoun\n        return False\n    if any(generic in lemma.split() for generic in GENERIC_TERMS):\n        return False\n    return True","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for sentence in sentences[0:10]:\n    sentence_tokenized = nlp(sentence['text'])\n    for chunk in sentence_tokenized.noun_chunks:\n        if is_valid_chunk(chunk):\n            print(clean_chunk(chunk))","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Pipeline","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"There are some issues still, but let's create a pipeline to run the full chapter and map the duplicates, then we choose the top candidate concepts that appear, for that we will count they appearances.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def run_pipeline_concepts(sentences):\n    texts = [s['text'] for s in sentences]\n    docs = list(nlp.pipe(texts, batch_size=32))\n    \n    concept_map = {}\n    \n    for sentence, doc in zip(sentences, docs):\n        for chunk in doc.noun_chunks:\n            if is_valid_chunk(chunk):\n                concept = clean_chunk(chunk)\n                if concept not in concept_map:\n                    concept_map[concept] = {'pages': [], 'count': 0}\n                concept_map[concept]['pages'].append(sentence['page'])\n                concept_map[concept]['count'] += 1\n\n    return concept_map\n    \n\ndef print_top_k_concepts(concept_map, k):\n    sorted_concepts = sorted(concept_map.items(), key=lambda x: x[1]['count'], reverse=True)\n    print(f'Unique concept found: {len(concept_map)}')\n    print(f'\\nTop {k} concepts:')\n    for concept, data in sorted_concepts[:k]:\n        print(f\" {concept:<20} count: {data['count']} pages: {set(data['pages'])}\")\n\ncm = run_pipeline_concepts(sentences)\nprint_top_k_concepts(cm, 20)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Ok it was a good run, we could capture core concepts such as :\n- digital system\n- digital computer\n- binary code\n- decimal number\n\nAll relevant to this chapter, but we see a lot of candidates being either **generics** as:\n- *number*\n- *value*\n\nwhich has some relevance in why they appear but it's not a good candidate, and nouns that are related to the **textbook structure** like:\n- figure\n- table\n- chapter\n\nAs there latter are related to noise we would filter them as we did with generic terms.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"BOOK_STRUCTURE = {\"figure\", \"table\", \"chapter\", \"section\", \"page\", \"appendix\", \"example\"}\nGENERIC_TERMS  = {'think', 'term', 'way', 'type', 'kind', 'part', 'example', \"number\", \"value\", \"datum\", \"base\", \"output\", \"code\", \"power\", \"information\"}\n\ndef is_valid_chunk(chunk):\n    lemma = clean_chunk(chunk)\n    if len(lemma) < 4:\n        return False\n    if chunk.root.pos_ == \"PRON\":\n        return False\n    if any(w in BOOK_STRUCTURE for w in lemma.split()):\n        return False\n    if lemma in GENERIC_TERMS:\n        return False\n    return True","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"concept_filtered = run_pipeline_concepts(sentences)\nprint_top_k_concepts(concept_filtered,20)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"A lot of the captured concepts are real concepts, there still generic words such as:\n- computer (covered by digital computer)\n- system (covered by digital system)\n- range\n- result\n\nAnd nouns that are related to an example like *temperature* that spans over some pages but mentioned a lot on them.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"SINGLE_WORD_GENERIC = {'computer', 'system', 'result', 'range', 'remainder'}\n\ndef is_valid_chunk(chunk):\n    lemma = clean_chunk(chunk)\n    if len(lemma) < 4:\n        return False\n    if chunk.root.pos_ == \"PRON\":\n        return False\n    if any(w in BOOK_STRUCTURE for w in lemma.split()):\n        return False\n    if lemma in GENERIC_TERMS:\n        return False\n    if lemma in SINGLE_WORD_GENERIC:\n        return False\n    return True","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"concept_filtered = run_pipeline_concepts(sentences)\nprint_top_k_concepts(concept_filtered,20)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We still have some issues but we got the core idea, let's test using textrank","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"!pip install pytextrank --quiet\n\nimport spacy\nimport pytextrank\n\ndef run_pipeline_textrank(sentences):\n    nlp_tr = spacy.load(\"en_core_web_sm\")  # fresh pipeline\n    nlp_tr.add_pipe(\"textrank\")         \n    \n    sample_text = \" \".join([s['text'] for s in sentences[0:10]])\n    doc = nlp_tr(sample_text)\n    return doc\n\nsample_concepts_textrank = run_pipeline_textrank(sentences)\n\nprint(\"Top ranked concepts:\")\nfor phrase in sample_concepts_textrank._.phrases[:15]:\n    print(f\"  {phrase.text:<30} score: {phrase.rank:.4f}  count: {phrase.count}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We see that textrank weights the concepts and captures them a lot better with little to no work, still has captured some duplicated or same meaning concepts or noise as *other things* or *digital systems* and *digital system design*. Let's elaborate on this and see where it lead us","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def run_pipeline_textrank(sentences):\n    nlp_tr = spacy.load(\"en_core_web_sm\")  # fresh pipeline\n    nlp_tr.add_pipe(\"textrank\")\n    \n    full_text = \" \".join([s['text'] for s in sentences])\n    page_lookup = {s['text']: s['page'] for s in sentences}\n    \n    doc = nlp_tr(full_text)\n    \n    # Build concept map from TextRank phrases\n    concept_map_tr = {}\n    \n    for phrase in doc._.phrases:\n        concept = phrase.text.lower().strip()\n        \n        concept = re.sub(r'^(the|a|an|this|these)\\s+', '', concept)\n        concept = re.sub(r'[^\\w\\s-]', '', concept).strip()\n        \n        if len(concept) < 4:\n            continue\n        if any(w in BOOK_STRUCTURE for w in concept.split()):\n            continue\n            \n            \n        concept_map_tr[concept] = {\n            \"score\": phrase.rank,\n            \"count\": phrase.count,\n            \"pages\": set()  \n        }\n    \n    return concept_map_tr\n\n\ndef print_top_k_textrank(concept_map_tr, k=20):\n    sorted_concepts = sorted(\n        concept_map_tr.items(),\n        key=lambda x: x[1]['score'],\n        reverse=True\n    )\n    \n    print(f\"Unique concepts found: {len(concept_map_tr)}\")\n    print(f\"\\nTop {k} concepts:\")\n    for concept, data in sorted_concepts[:k]:\n        print(f\"  {concept:<35} score: {data['score']:.4f}  count: {data['count']}\")\n\n# Run both pipelines\nconcepts_nc = run_pipeline_concepts(sentences)\nconcepts_tr = run_pipeline_textrank(sentences)\n\n# Print both\nprint(\"=== NOUN CHUNKS ===\")\nprint_top_k_concepts(concepts_nc, 20)\n\nprint(\"\\n=== TEXTRANK ===\")\nprint_top_k_textrank(concepts_tr, 20)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"After comparing both of them textrank captures techincal terms better but still captures modifiers as *other* and *more*.\n\nThe Noun Chunks strategy avoids these modifiers via our custom filters we applied, resulting in less noise and enabling page tracking for concept appareances. However, it lacks a clear importance score.\n\nBut there is one place where concept are usually showcase for the reader, thats the headers, Could extract entities from headers instead?","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def extract_headings(pdf_path, size_threshold=14):\n    doc = fitz.open(pdf_path)\n    headings = []\n    \n    for page_num, page in enumerate(doc):\n        blocks = page.get_text(\"dict\")[\"blocks\"]\n        for block in blocks:\n            if block[\"type\"] != 0:\n                continue\n            for line in block[\"lines\"]:\n                line_text = \"\".join(span[\"text\"] for span in line[\"spans\"]).strip()\n                line_size = max(span[\"size\"] for span in line[\"spans\"])\n                \n                if line_size > size_threshold and line_text:\n                    headings.append({\n                        \"text\": line_text,\n                        \"page\": page_num + 1,\n                        \"size\": round(line_size, 1)\n                    })\n    return headings\n\nextracted_headings = extract_headings('/kaggle/input/datasets/nahuelfuentes/textbook-example/textbook-chapter-1.pdf',10)\nfor heading in extracted_headings:\n    print(heading)\n","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We see some concepts emerging in the heading but as we are capturing headings solely on the text span they have we have a lot of noise related to either book structure like *REFERENCES* or *PROBLEMS* and noise from examples that, i assume, come from having a large font size.\n\nInstead on relying on an LLM for filtering these we will continue using Regular Expression to clean up and validate.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import re\n\nBOOK_HEADING_PATTERN = re.compile(\n    r'^(problems|references|exercises|summary|index|appendix|contents|example|figure|table)\\b', \n    re.IGNORECASE\n)\n\nMATH_EQUATION_PATTERN = re.compile(r'[\\+=/\\*]|\\b\\d+\\s*[\\+=/\\*]\\s*\\d+')\n\nPREFIX_NUMBER_PATTERN = re.compile(\n    r'^((chap(ter)?|sec(tion)?|\\d+)\\s*\\d*[\\.:-]*\\s*)+', \n    re.IGNORECASE\n)\n\ndef clean_and_validate_heading(heading_text):\n    cleaned = \" \".join(heading_text.split()).strip()\n    \n    # Drop purely numeric lines or empty strings (noise artifacts)\n    if not cleaned or cleaned.isdigit() or len(cleaned) <= 3:\n        return None\n        \n    # Drop headings that match structural book layout noise\n    if BOOK_HEADING_PATTERN.match(cleaned):\n        return None\n\n    # Skip if it contains math operators or equation remanants as it could be an artifcat\n    if MATH_EQUATION_PATTERN.search(cleaned):\n        return None\n        \n    # Strip numbering prefixes (e.g., \"1.3 Introduction\" -> \"Introduction\")\n    cleaned = PREFIX_NUMBER_PATTERN.sub('', cleaned).strip()\n    \n    # Double check after stripping prefixes that we didn't end up with an empty string or tiny fragment\n    if len(cleaned) <= 3:\n        return None\n        \n    return cleaned\n\ncleaned_headings = []\n\nfor item in extracted_headings:\n    text = item['text'] if isinstance(item, dict) else item \n    \n    cleaned_text = clean_and_validate_heading(text)\n    \n    if cleaned_text:\n        if isinstance(item, dict):\n            # Keep original metadata like page number, but update the text span\n            cleaned_item = item.copy()\n            cleaned_item['text'] = cleaned_text\n            cleaned_headings.append(cleaned_item)\n        else:\n            cleaned_headings.append(cleaned_text)\n\nprint(f\"Original headings: {len(extracted_headings)}\")\nprint(f\"Cleaned valid headings: {len(cleaned_headings)}\")\nfor heading in cleaned_headings:\n    print(heading)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"We filtered a lot of the noise, still we have some examples artifacts related to examples or book structure we could filter, but we will continue as even though we could do it right now the concepts aren't all in there and also aren't clearly separated, it's an option we could explore later to have concepts in the heading as a confidence source of them.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Our regex filter reduced the initial layout extraction from **110 items down to 26 items**, leaving us the core concepts of the chapter (e.g., `Digital systems`, `Binary Numebrs`, `Octal and Hexadecimal Numbers`)\n\nThere are still some artifacts related to broken multi-line like `Digital Systems` and `and Information` and Math traces such as `E 4 6 14 4 6`.\n\nWhile these headings serve as a phenomenal high-confidence data source, they present an extra NLP challenge: **sub-concept fragmentation**. For example:\n\n- `ABSTRACTION LAYERS IN COMPUTER SYSTEM DESIGN` needs to be decomposed to `ABSTRACTION` or `Abstraction Layers`.\n- `Octal and Hexadecimal Numbers` actually represents two distinct semantic concepts: `Octal` and `Hexadecimal`.\n\nThey are a great source and alternative we could dive more in the future to work with other strategies.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Relationships\n\nFor capturing relationships we will use two techinques: \n- **bootstrapping** using our concepts as seeds to automatially capture the most frequent relationship betweent them are\n- **Dependency Parsing** capture the relations based on what we see in the text\n\nThey could complement each other","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Bootstrapping\n\nThe general idea of bootstrapping is like a `GREP`, we match the strings that have our concepts, then we see what's between them, relationships will emerge as more common they are.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from collections import Counter\nimport re\n\ndef get_text_between(sentence, concept_a, concept_b):\n    pattern = rf'{re.escape(concept_a)}(.+?){re.escape(concept_b)}'\n    match = re.search(pattern, sentence.lower())\n    if match:\n        return match.group(1).strip()\n    return None\n\ndef bootstrap_relations(sentence, concept_map, top_k=20, min_count=2):\n    top_concepts = [c for c, _ in sorted(\n        concept_map.items(),\n        key=lambda x: x[1]['count'],\n        reverse=True\n    )[:top_k]]\n\n    between_patterns = Counter()\n    relation_examples = {}\n\n    for sentence in sentences:\n        text = sentence['text'].lower()\n\n        for i, concept_a in enumerate(top_concepts):\n            for concept_b in top_concepts[i+1:]:\n                if concept_a not in text or concept_b not in text:\n                    continue\n\n                between = get_text_between(text, concept_a, concept_b)\n                if not between:\n                    between = get_text_between(text, concept_b, concept_a)\n                    if between:\n                        concept_a, concept_b = concept_b, concept_a\n\n                if between and 2 < len(between.split()) < 4:\n                    between_patterns[between] += 1\n\n                    if between not in relation_examples:\n                        relation_examples[between] = []\n                    if len(relation_examples[between]) < 3:\n                        relation_examples[between].append(f'({concept_a}) [{between}] ({concept_b})')\n\n    print('Most frequent patterns between concepts:\\n')\n    for pattern, count in between_patterns.most_common(20):\n        if count >= min_count:\n            print(f\" '{pattern}' -> count: {count}\")\n            for ex in relation_examples[pattern]:\n                print(f\"    e.g. {ex}\")\n            print()\n            \n    return between_patterns, relation_examples","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"patterns, examples = bootstrap_relations(sentences, concepts_nc, top_k=50, min_count=2)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"As we store the lemmas of our concepts we run into problems when we came across plurales of modified words of them, in this example \n> voltage -> voltages\n\nSo instead of capturing *for a* as a relationship we capture *s for a* as the lemma doesn't have that s, also in the other end we have repetition for the same stuff, digit and digital circuits it's a by product of matching the lemma of a single and compound concept","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def get_concept_pattern(concept):\n    base = re.escape(concept)\n    # Match base form + optional s, es, ed, ing\n    return rf'\\b{base}(?:s|es|ed|ing)?\\b'\n\ndef get_text_between(sentence, concept_a, concept_b):\n    pattern_a = get_concept_pattern(concept_a)\n    pattern_b = get_concept_pattern(concept_b)\n    \n    pattern = rf'({pattern_a})(.+?)({pattern_b})'\n    match = re.search(pattern, sentence.lower())\n    if match:\n        return match.group(2).strip()  # group 2 = between\n    return None","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"patterns, examples = bootstrap_relations(sentences, concepts_nc, top_k=50, min_count=2)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Even though we fix it we can't use really the core of bootstrapping as frequencies in one chapter of a notebook that we are using is limited, making patter frequency unreliable.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Dependecy Parsing","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"test_sentence = sentences[5]['text']\nprint(test_sentence)\n\ndoc = nlp(test_sentence)\ndisplacy.render(doc, style=\"dep\", jupyter=True)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Patters that emerge form this analysis:\n- is (e.g. computer is system) `nsubj`+`AUX`+`attr`\n- follow (e.g. system can follow sequence) `nsubj`+`VERB`+`dobj`\n- called (e.g. sequence called program) `oprd`\n- operates (e.g. program operates on data) `nsubj`+`VERB`+`prep`+`pobj`\n\nWe will try to extract them","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def get_chunk(doc, token):\n    for chunk in doc.noun_chunks:\n        if chunk.root == token:\n            return clean_chunk(chunk)\n    return token.lemma_.lower()\n\ndef extract_relations(doc):\n    triples = []\n\n    for token in doc:\n        if token.pos_ not in ('VERB','AUX'):\n            continue\n\n        subjects = [w for w in token.lefts\n                   if w.dep_ in ('nsubj','nsubjpass')]\n\n        # We apply the patterns\n\n        attrs = [w for w in token.rights\n                if w.dep_ == 'attr']\n\n        dobjects = [w for w in token.rights\n                   if w.dep_ == 'dobj']\n\n        oprds = [w for w in token.rights\n               if w.dep_ == 'oprd']\n        \n        preps = [w for w in token.rights\n                if w.dep_ == 'prep']\n\n        for subj in subjects:\n                    subj_chunk = get_chunk(doc, subj)\n\n                    for obj in dobjects:\n                        triples.append((subj_chunk, token.lemma_, get_chunk(doc, obj)))\n\n                    for attr in attrs:\n                        triples.append((subj_chunk, 'is_a', get_chunk(doc, attr)))\n\n                    for oprd in oprds:\n                        triples.append((subj_chunk, 'also_known_as', get_chunk(doc, oprd)))\n\n                    for prep in preps:\n                        pobjs = [w for w in prep.rights if w.dep_ == 'pobj']\n                        for pobj in pobjs:\n                            relation = f\"{token.lemma_}_{prep.text}\"\n                            triples.append((subj_chunk, relation, get_chunk(doc, pobj)))\n\n    return triples","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"test = sentences[5]['text']\ndoc = nlp(test)\ntriples = extract_relations(doc)\nfor t in triples:\n    print(t)","metadata":{"trusted":true},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Rig